1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,970
This video shows you how to put a reverse proxy in front of your leader,

2
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:08,830
so it serves your organization's certificate.

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:12,171
Your laptop trusts your organization's authority,

4
00:00:12,171 --> 00:00:15,230
but not the leader's own, so Chrome warns.

5
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:20,968
On the leader's host, your organization's certificate and key are in the certs folder.

6
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:26,774
The proxy is Caddy.

7
00:00:26,774 --> 00:00:30,550
It serves that certificate, and forwards to smeltr.

8
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,391
Add the proxy to compose.

9
00:00:32,391 --> 00:00:34,756
The leader turns its own TLS off,

10
00:00:34,756 --> 00:00:39,350
and no longer publishes a port, so only the proxy reaches it.

11
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:40,971
Start it.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,585
Open smeltr again.

13
00:00:45,585 --> 00:00:47,610
There's no warning now.

14
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:48,914
Sign in.

15
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:55,070
One worker has gone silent: the one on the other machine.

16
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:57,760
On the worker's host, its log says why.

17
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:01,880
It doesn't trust the certificate the proxy presents.

18
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:08,044
Start it again, giving it your organization's authority to verify the leader with.

19
00:01:08,044 --> 00:01:12,270
Its state volume keeps its identity, so it doesn't enroll again.

20
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:16,156
Back in smeltr, both workers are online.

21
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:19,340
Check on learning.

22
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:22,541
The leader turned its own TLS off.

23
00:01:22,541 --> 00:01:25,083
Why is that safe here?

24
00:01:29,483 --> 00:01:31,109
It publishes no port,

25
00:01:31,109 --> 00:01:37,127
so other machines reach it only through the proxy, which serves HTTPS.

26
00:01:38,027 --> 00:01:43,262
Why did the second worker go silent, while the first stayed online?

27
00:01:47,662 --> 00:01:52,260
It met the proxy's certificate, from an authority it didn't trust yet.

28
00:01:52,260 --> 00:01:56,670
The first runs inside the leader, and reaches it directly.

29
00:01:57,570 --> 00:01:59,062
That's Trusted HTTPS.

30
00:01:59,062 --> 00:02:01,904
You trusted your leader's own authority,

31
00:02:01,904 --> 00:02:06,624
ordered a certificate over ACME, and put a proxy in front.
